<!--
  Device HAL manifest (VINTF) for the m5c on LineageOS 16.0.

  Why this file exists (peripherals lane, 2026-09-03).  The m5c port has no
  DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE, so /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml is absent and
  hwservicemanager answers getTransport() with EMPTY for every vendor HAL.
  Most clients survive that: system/libhidl's getRawServiceInternal() only
  warns on an undeclared interface and still tries hwbinder, then passthrough.

  Wi-Fi is the exception.  frameworks/opt/net/wifi HalDeviceManager decides
  whether a vendor HAL exists at all by asking the manifest directly:

      isSupportedInternal():
          IServiceManager.getTransport(IWifi.kInterfaceName, "default")
              != Transport.EMPTY

  With no manifest that returns EMPTY, WifiNative logs
  "Vendor Hal not supported, ignoring start", IWifi.start() is never called,
  and hardware/interfaces/wifi/1.2/default/wifi_mode_controller.cpp:63
  (driver_tool_->LoadDriver() -> wifi_load_driver() -> "1" into
  WIFI_DRIVER_STATE_CTRL_PARAM = /dev/wmtWifi) never runs.  The MTK combo
  driver therefore never powers up, wlan0 is never created, and wificond
  reports "No interface is found".  Measured on the live device 2026-09-03
  13:36 (M5C_LOS16_PERIPHERALS_MEASURE_20260903.md).

  Scope is deliberately narrow.  A HAL declared here but not actually served
  hangs its client (the m681 light@2.0 lesson), so this manifest lists only
  interfaces observed registered in lshal on the running device.  Every other
  vendor HAL on the m5c already resolves through the EMPTY-transport fallback
  and needs no entry; add one only together with runtime proof that the
  service answers.
-->
<!--
  2026-09-25, FULL TREBLE (branch lineage-20-treble,
  meizu-fleet/designs/TREBLE_M5C_20260924.md).  The narrow-scope rule above
  was written for a NON-Treble image, where an undeclared HAL still resolves
  through the EMPTY-transport fallback.  With PRODUCT_FULL_TREBLE the build
  sets PRODUCT_ENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST, and that fallback is gone:
    * hwservicemanager refuses to register a service that is not declared,
      so an undeclared HAL is simply absent;
    * a declared HAL is waited for, so a declared HAL whose service cannot
      come up still hangs its client (the m681 light@2.0 lesson stands).
  Hence the rule now is: declare exactly the HIDL HALs whose service AND
  implementation are in this image.  Declared below, each with its impl:
    composer 2.1      composer@2.1-service (hwcomposer.mt6737m = forge_hwc is
                      NOT ported yet; SurfaceFlinger cannot run without a
                      composer either way — display lane)
    allocator 2.0     allocator@2.0-impl/-service + gralloc.mt6737m (blob)
    mapper 2.0        mapper@2.0-impl, passthrough (sphal; closure clean)
    sensors 1.0       sensors@1.0-impl + sensors.mt6737m (blob)
    light 2.0         light@2.0-impl + lights.mt6737m (blob)
    vibrator 1.0      vibrator@1.0-impl + vibrator.default (AOSP)
    memtrack 1.0      memtrack@1.0-impl + memtrack.mt6737m (blob)
    camera.provider   provider@2.4-impl legacy/0 + camera.mt6737m (blob)
    bluetooth 1.1     bluetooth@1.1-service; libbt-vendor is looked up only
                      at initialize(), so the service itself registers (the
                      missing 64-bit libbt-vendor is fleet wall (a));
                      2026-09-28: 32-bit copy of the service, bluetooth/
    media.omx 1.0     media.omx@1.0-service (IOmx + IOmxStore)
    wifi 1.2          unchanged from before, see NOT WIRED 2 in device.mk
    audio 6.0         audio@6.0-impl + effect@6.0-impl under
                      android.hardware.audio.service; REQUIRED by the FCM 3
                      matrix (checkvintf rc=65 without it).  The factory
                      registers; audio.primary.mt6737m still misses libmedia
                      and libpowermanager in the vendor namespace (shim lane).
  Declared by their own fragments, not here: health@2.1 (health/2.1/default
  Android.bp:83), gatekeeper@1.0 software, drm@1.4 clearkey (both REQUIRED by
  FCM 3 as well), keymint, cas, hostapd, wpa_supplicant (AIDL).
  Deliberately NOT declared (service present, implementation absent — a
  declaration would hang the client, and for gnss that client is
  system_server): gnss 1.0 (gps.mt6737m not ported), radio (mtk-ril.so has
  no RIL_Init).
  target-level 3: Android 13 carries FCM levels 3..7 only; 3 is what m95
  runs (device/meizu/m95/manifest.xml).
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device" target-level="3">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.composer</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.1</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IComposer</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.allocator</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IAllocator</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.mapper</name>
        <transport arch="32+64">passthrough</transport>
        <version>2.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IMapper</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.sensors</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ISensors</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.light</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ILight</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.vibrator</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IVibrator</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.memtrack</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IMemtrack</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.camera.provider</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.4</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ICameraProvider</name>
            <instance>legacy/0</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>6.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IDevicesFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio.effect</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>6.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IEffectsFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.bluetooth</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.1</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IBluetoothHci</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.media.omx</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IOmx</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
        <interface>
            <name>IOmxStore</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <!--
      Radio (2026-09-28): the MTK Oreo libril of ril/ registers IRadio,
      IOemHook and ISap for slot1 and slot2 (ril_service.cpp and
      sap_service.cpp registerService, SIM_COUNT 2); hwservicemanager refuses
      a registration that the manifest does not declare.
    -->
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.radio</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IRadio</name>
            <instance>slot1</instance>
            <instance>slot2</instance>
        </interface>
        <interface>
            <name>ISap</name>
            <instance>slot1</instance>
            <instance>slot2</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.radio.deprecated</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IOemHook</name>
            <instance>slot1</instance>
            <instance>slot2</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <!--
      android.hardware.wifi is declared by the fragment of the HAL package
      itself (android.hardware.wifi@1.0-service.xml, IWifi 1.6,
      hardware/interfaces/wifi/1.6/default/Android.mk:100), installed together
      with the service (device.mk, 2026-09-28).  A second entry here is the
      m95 trap (device/meizu/m95/manifest.xml:294-297): duplicate HAL ->
      "has a conflict" -> the whole vendor manifest rejected.
    -->
    <!--
      android.hardware.wifi.supplicant is deliberately NOT declared here
      (2026-09-24, lesson of m95 161682f).  The Android 13 wpa_supplicant of
      this tree serves only the AIDL ISupplicant (CONFIG_CTRL_IFACE_AIDL,
      external/wpa_supplicant_8/wpa_supplicant/android.config:332) and declares
      it itself in its own VINTF fragment,
      /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.wifi.supplicant.xml
      (wpa_supplicant/Android.mk:1827).  The HIDL 1.1 entry that stood here
      would be a declared-but-unserved HAL (see the rule above).  The service
      itself is in rootdir/etc/init/init.m5c.wifi.rc.
    -->
</manifest>
